WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.240
Since Apex Legends launched in 2019, the&nbsp;
Respawn team has added a tonne to the game:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.000
new legends, maps, abilities, lore,&nbsp;
you name it. With so much to dig into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.760
here are 9 things you might&nbsp;
not know about Apex Legends.&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.600
Octane, the high-energy, death-defying stuntman&nbsp;
was first added back in season one of Apex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.680
but you might not know that his origins actually&nbsp;
lie in the Titanfall 2 speedrunning community.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.520
If you played Titanfall 2, you might remember&nbsp;
its Gauntlet: a replayable tutorial area with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:41.200
a tantalizing leaderboard that just begged you to&nbsp;
try it over and over again. Cash Mayo rose to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.280
challenge, completing the Gauntlet in 12 seconds&nbsp;
flat by using frag grenades to give themself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.400
a speed boost. The team at Respawn were&nbsp;
clearly impressed, and incorporated this into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.000
Octane’s stuntman backstory: he set the course&nbsp;
record on the Gauntlet by using a grenade to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.840
propel himself across the finish line, resulting&nbsp;
in him losing both legs. And if you’re wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:10.240
if Cash Mayo’s 12-second Gauntlet record still&nbsp;
stands, it doesn’t. Cash Mayo has now managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:16.080
complete the feat in a little over 10 seconds.
If there’s one thing I know about Mirage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:27.040
it’s that he loves pork chops. So much so, in&nbsp;
fact, that his recipe is included on one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.960
game’s loading screens. Andrew Rea, more commonly&nbsp;
known for his YouTube series Binging With Babish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:40.960
made Mirage’s pork chops exactly how the recipe&nbsp;
calls for, adding 15 GRAMS of ground cloves and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:51.760
additional 15 grams of ground cinnamon. It doesn’t&nbsp;
just taste like cloves, it burns like cloves.&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.720
Turns out that Manny Hagopian, the&nbsp;
writer responsible for Mirage’s recipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.560
made a slight error when converting&nbsp;
the recipe to the metric system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.920
Manny came up with a revised recipe, which&nbsp;
Babish made and gave his seal of approval to.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:12.160
Mirage isn’t the only Legend with a go-to recipe,&nbsp;
Lifeline’s cookies and Mad Maggie’s Warrior Feast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:20.720
are also both supposed to be delicious.
Back in 2020, Respawn ran the , and one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.480
limited-time modes let you play the game in 3rd&nbsp;
person. While the mode was short-lived, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:34.080
still play in the third person in the firing range&nbsp;
just by following some simple steps. The elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.280
in the room with Apex is that it’s made by the&nbsp;
Titanfall team, it’s set in the same universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.760
as Titanfall, some characters and loadouts are&nbsp;
even FROM Titanfall, so how come there are no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.040
Titans in the game? Well, the Apex team has&nbsp;
tried to add them to the game multiple times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.240
internally, but the Titans cause balance issues&nbsp;
that the team hasn’t found the right solution to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:02.240
like how do you make a battle royale map suitable&nbsp;
for the giant Titans? How do you account for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.360
Titans’ vast health pool? While the team&nbsp;
has gone on record to say never say never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.800
with regard to adding them to the game, they’re&nbsp;
not on the roadmap right now. So Titans may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.160
have made it to Apex, but plenty of characters&nbsp;
from the Titanfall games have. Honestly, I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.880
make a whole video about these connections, but&nbsp;
here are some key ones you might have missed.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.720
Valkyrie, for example, is the daughter&nbsp;
of Viper, you may remember him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.840
as being the pilot of the jerk Titan&nbsp;
that ripped off BT’s arm in Titanfall 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.600
Valkyrie, real name Kairi Imahara, even&nbsp;
had fellow legend Rampart use the flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.640
core from her dad’s Titan to make the&nbsp;
jetpack she uses for the Apex Games. v

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.600
Mirage is the nephew of Droz, who&nbsp;
helped Cooper and BT hijack the IMS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.320
Malta and provided covering fire when&nbsp;
Cooper went head to head with Viper.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.680
Three Legends were previously&nbsp;
Titan pilots: Wraith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.080
Ash, and Newcastle. While technically&nbsp;
Valkyrie has piloted a titan before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.520
she didn’t complete her training to become&nbsp;
a fully-fledged pilot. Octane owns a Titan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.400
but he isn’t a pilot. He just keeps one&nbsp;
covered in graffiti in his massive house.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.200
Wraith isn’t from the Titanfall games,&nbsp;
but she’s got a cool link to the Titanfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.320
games nonetheless. She’s from an alternate&nbsp;
dimension where the bad guys from Titanfall 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:26.240
actually won–this ties into her in-game abilities&nbsp;
to be able to hear warning voices, which are her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.920
from other dimensions, and of course, her ability&nbsp;
to use a dimensional rift to get out of trouble.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.160
While there are obviously many Titanfall&nbsp;
Easter Eggs to enjoy in Apex Legends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.000
references to other games are hidden throughout.&nbsp;
For example, Wattson’s Strange Attractor skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.040
is clearly a reference to Samus from&nbsp;
Metroid, even down to the teal bracers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.640
mimicking Samus’ arm canon. Crypto’s Devil’s&nbsp;
Advocate skin, is a Devil May Cry reference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.520
with the red coat and black shirt looking&nbsp;
a hell of a lot like Dante’s usual get up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.040
Pathfinder has a banner pose called “For the&nbsp;
Birds”, where he gets attacked by a raven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.080
clearly an ode to Overwatch’s Bastion,&nbsp;
who has a bird friend named Ganymede.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.360
It even translates to weapons: the Mastiff&nbsp;
has a legendary skin called “Warp Zone”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.920
the name itself is a reference to shortcuts&nbsp;
from Super Mario Bros, but the skin is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.000
teeming with references to video games, from NES&nbsp;
buttons and cartridge slot to a USB connector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.200
and a little screen - while this character&nbsp;
looks like a little Mega Man, it was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:36.240
confirmed by Apex Senior Producer Josh Medina to&nbsp;
be pixel Jack Cooper from Titan Toons, a Titanfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:43.840
cartoon series made by Respawn animator Moy Parra.
Back at the 2019 Game Awards, Mirage appeared in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:50.000
“video call” with host Geoff Keighley to announce&nbsp;
that year’s Winter Event: Mirage’s Holo-Day Bash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.800
While it may have looked like it was&nbsp;
pre-recorded and made specifically for the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:13.120
it was actually done live. Mirage’s actor Roger&nbsp;
Craig Smith was decked in a performance capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.960
outfit and was speaking to Geoff who was on stage&nbsp;
at the Microsoft Theatre across the street. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.920
stunt was a collaboration between Respawn and&nbsp;
a creative technology studio called the Mill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.120
where they utilised real-time rendered shooting&nbsp;
tech to shoot and animate the skit. Here’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.800
weird one that you might not know: many of the&nbsp;
Legends may look spry and energetic, but several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:42.400
of them are over one hundred years old. Horizon&nbsp;
for example is 37, but she was trapped in a black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:50.640
hole for a few decades, making her roughly 125&nbsp;
years old. Ash is 121 and also spent a few years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.360
with her head shattered in another dimension,&nbsp;
but that’s a story for a different video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:03.040
and Revenant is an astonishing 313. The&nbsp;
youngest legend is Rampart, she’s only 21.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.160
Okay, this one’s cheating because it’s a&nbsp;
Titanfall easter egg, but an Apex dev helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.560
bring it to light, so I’m counting it. A Titanfall&nbsp;
2 Easter Egg went unnoticed for over five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.400
until Respawn’s Narrative Historian David Bartle&nbsp;
dropped a couple of hints on his Twitter account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.480
as to its location. Turns out in Titanfall 2’s&nbsp;
infamous time travelling Effect and Cause mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:34.240
if you time swapped eighty-eight times outside a&nbsp;
certain door, the room changes into a… weird one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:40.240
where a dead soldier’s body lies on the floor,&nbsp;
while another is …doing the splits between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.200
kitchen countertops. Definitely a bit of a&nbsp;
weird one, but one that remained hidden for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.880
an awfully long time. It was the creation&nbsp;
of Respawn senior designer Jake Keating.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.600
So there you have it, 9 things you may not&nbsp;
know about Apex Legends. For more on the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.240
make sure you check out Jordan’s weekly&nbsp;
column Hot Drop over on GameSpot.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.520
or subscribe to the channel for more features like&nbsp;
this one. If you’ve got any Apex tidbits, drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:10.000
them in the comments below, or come find me on&nbsp;
Twitter, I’m @lucyjamesgames. Catch ya next time.

